      "text_raw": "For low supply voltages, it\nis difficult to maintain the\ninput common-mode volt-\nage constant during the\nswitching. In order to sup-\npress this change in\ncommon-mode input volt-\nage, several solutions exist.\nIn the top one, a capacitor\nC is added, connected to\nLs\nthe supply lines. It acts as a\nDC level shifter, as pre-\nviously described.\nIn the solution at the\nbottom, the input resistor R\nand sampling capacitor C\nS\nare doubled, allowing the cancellation of the input common-mode voltage. This results in less\nkT/C noise.",
      "text": "For low supply voltages, it is difficult to maintain the input common-mode voltage constant during the switching. In order to suppress this change in common-mode input voltage, several solutions exist. In the top one, a capacitor C is added, connected to Ls the supply lines. It acts as a DC level shifter, as previously described. In the solution at the bottom, the input resistor R and sampling capacitor C S are doubled, allowing the cancellation of the input common-mode voltage. This results in less kT/C noise."
    }
